      "message": "\u003e Can this be at line 163?\n\nNo, we only want the interrupt to be enabled when the system is in S0. (see the comment above chipset_watchdog_interrupt). \n\n\u003e What causes this interrupt to be disabled after sysjump?\n\nexti_events in chip/stm32/gpio.c is not restored, so the EC does not know which interrupt to call.\n\n\twhile (pending) {\n\t\tbit \u003d get_next_bit(\u0026pending);\n\t\tsignal \u003d exti_events[bit];\n\t\tif (bit \u003d\u003d 2)\n\t\t\tccprints(\"bit %d signal %d\", bit, signal);\n\t\tif (signal \u003c GPIO_IH_COUNT)\n\t\t\tgpio_irq_handlers[signal](signal);\n\t}\n\nIn RO:\n2019-06-24 15:13:40 [323.468323 bit 2 signal 7]\nIn RW:\n2019-06-24 15:17:36 [65.081743 bit 2 signal 0]\nso this ends up calling tcpc_alert_event\n2019-06-24 15:21:45 [56.694080 tcpc_alert_event 0]\n\nthat\u0027s... bad.\n\nI think it\u0027s ok that interrupts are not restored on sysjump, but we shouldn\u0027t be calling a random interrupt function.\n\nhttps://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id\u003d977950",
